#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124255 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "37734955-23b7-52d1-881d-0c77f672c35a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3162" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y, z)= xyz+y^2.$ Find the best linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b, z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(3, -1, 1)= -2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{x}(3,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz+2y\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{y}(3,-1,1)=1 \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{z}(3,-1,1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(3, -1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(3,-1,1) & =⟨-1,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-1,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2-(x-3)+(y+1)-3(z-1) \\\\  & =-2-x+3+y+1-3z+3 \\\\  & =5-x+y-3z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "839eb8fd-39b8-5f1f-b833-7134d39cdf2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8674" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,1) = 1, \\quad f_x(x,y) = 2xy, \\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.1, 0.9).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $f_x(1,1)$ and $f_y(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1)(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =(1)^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx 1 + \\langle 2, 1 \\rangle \\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.1, 0.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.1$ and $y=0.9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.1,0.9) & ≈1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.1-1,0.9-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.1,-0.1⟩ \\\\  & =1+0.2-0.1 \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124610 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "54ac5be6-5b6a-58a3-a960-0d9f18e7fc88" :question/math-academy-id "e-8692" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,5)=2, \\quad  f_x(1,2,5)=4, \\quad  f_y(1,2,5)=1, \\quad f_z(1,2,5)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,2.02,4.95).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b, c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,5)+∇f(1,2,5)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,5)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,5),{f}_{y}(1,2,5),{f}_{z}(1,2,5)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.01,$ $y=2.02,$ and $z=4.95$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,2.02,4.95) & ≈2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,2.02-2,4.95-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =2+0.04+0.02+0.1 \\\\  & =2.16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079adc53-d9b0-579c-bafb-1a2fa1933186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263069} {:answer/id #uuid "1721e845-6b19-541d-8c83-55ebba410dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈3+x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263217} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3425c4-3b21-54cc-95e4-cc3c5d99fb99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-1+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263946} {:answer/id #uuid "84007c55-fca6-5d28-9193-9bcd0dd075ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈1-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264133} {:answer/id #uuid "8d90d300-7c94-5dd8-936d-19bc88df333e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "079adc53-d9b0-579c-bafb-1a2fa1933186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263069} :answer-field/id #uuid "417bd589-801b-52b9-b3ec-42fe3a9a321b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3615abea-583e-5e5f-b0c4-561023b77ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-129745" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-y.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,-1)+∇f(0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,-1)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =4x\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(0,-1)=0, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-1 &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(0,-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,-1) & =⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,-1)+∇f(0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+0-(y+1) \\\\  & ≈-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3996a57d-3ca8-524e-b713-371dae9f92c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈\\pi -1-x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420920} {:answer/id #uuid "783e5bf5-dd91-511c-81fe-eca19c21cfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420922} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0f50fc-3f9e-56b0-874f-3d40ee14ed33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-2-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420925} {:answer/id #uuid "df1e2f42-abc0-5025-a335-6ed1280fc719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈1-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420926} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a0590c-c153-5bd3-ab0a-b20fc19d4881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7a0590c-c153-5bd3-ab0a-b20fc19d4881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420927} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6db9cae-9882-5bc9-bc06-4b3bc77a65e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "051e613f-42d1-5b65-a3d0-8638cacfa921" :question/math-academy-id "q-36746" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,\\pi )+∇f(0,1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,1,\\pi )=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =y{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(0,1,\\pi )=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(0,1,\\pi )=0, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(0,1,\\pi )=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,1,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,1,\\pi ) & =⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,\\pi ),{f}_{y}(0,1,\\pi ),{f}_{z}(0,1,\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,\\pi )+∇f(0,1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124610 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5dad03-90e1-5272-a49f-915de2f550e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360940} {:answer/id #uuid "9673af85-358e-5a43-a43e-55b1d8617e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361507} {:answer/id #uuid "9beaf320-0512-5935-9d2e-a80f07d46e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361532} {:answer/id #uuid "dac4eb88-c8b4-5a95-83de-b77728c63bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361810} {:answer/id #uuid "ea97905b-554c-596d-9a9a-c2aa3847a0d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a5dad03-90e1-5272-a49f-915de2f550e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360940} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d62b665-005d-53f3-a00c-6c189d52c26a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f756b36e-1fa8-57e8-ad91-275a3866e1c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98340" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,2)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1,2)=-4,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1,2)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,-1,2)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,2)$ estimate the value of $f(1.04,-0.99,2.02).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,2)+∇f(1,-1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z-2⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1,2)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,2),{f}_{y}(1,-1,2),{f}_{z}(1,-1,2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z-2⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨-4,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.04,-0.99,2.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.04,$ $y=-0.99,$ and $z=2.02$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.04,-0.99,2.02) & ≈6+⟨-4,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1.04-1,-0.99+1,2.02-2⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨-4,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.04,0.01,0.02⟩ \\\\  & =6-0.16-0.01+0.06 \\\\  & =5.89\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
